WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.290
Hi.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:05.940
Today I want to talk about
learning a language from scratch.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:11.130
Remember, if you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe and you can click

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:12.870
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.070
And I am going to talk today
about how I use LingQ to

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.930
start a language from scratch.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:23.370
Now I have, this is kind of
the fourth in this series of

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.710
videos on studying effectively.

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:31.410
And one of the themes that you will have
heard throughout each of these videos is

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.660
the importance of listening and reading.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:40.170
So then people say, well, how can I listen
and read, uh, with a language that I

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.310
know nothing about, like from scratch,
how do you start listening and reading?

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.379
So I thought it would be useful to go off,
to go over some of the basic principles,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.629
what I do when I'm starting from scratch.

00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:54.660
Because very recently I
have started from scratch.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:01.980
In Persian in Arabic, in Turkish,
in Greek, uh, you know, I tend

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.830
to remember all the languages
that I've started from scratch.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:11.490
And the way I start from scratch is
that I use the Mini Stories and I

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:16.289
use the Mini Stories because there
is so much repetition in each story.

00:01:16.289 --> 00:01:20.835
So you have 60 stories where
within each story, there is lots

00:01:20.835 --> 00:01:26.535
of repetition, the same vocabulary
repeats five times within the story.

00:01:27.075 --> 00:01:33.975
One of the problems with more conventional
learner books, starter books is that

00:01:34.065 --> 00:01:41.625
while it may, it pretends to be easier in
fact, not a lot of the vocabulary repeats

00:01:41.625 --> 00:01:47.605
within the lesson, or even later on in the
book and we do need vocabulary to repeat

00:01:47.775 --> 00:01:51.975
if we're going to have a
chance to remember it also.

00:01:52.425 --> 00:01:58.365
Uh, I will demonstrate I'll show you
myself learning Indonesian from scratch.

00:01:58.735 --> 00:02:03.110
Now, I had some brief exposure to
Indonesian and because my wife and

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:06.330
I, uh, in 1975, we were in Bali.

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:11.910
So I remember a few words
like, um,..., "how much?"

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.939
Or ... "thank you".

00:02:14.939 --> 00:02:20.900
And I remember being in an airplane
and I heard ... which is ladies

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.880
and gentlemen, but that's about it.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:31.200
Uh, so I will demonstrate how I start
from scratch, uh, with the Mini Stories

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.540
and one of the things that I'll point
out in the Mini Stories is, while most

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:40.340
people go from lesson one to lesson two
to lesson three, the language is the

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.940
language, the language isn't so much
less than one lesson, two lesson three.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:47.810
It's not obvious that less than five
is more difficult than lesson one.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:53.210
The very high frequency words will
appear in lesson one and lesson two

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:56.780
and lesson three and lesson four and
lesson five and 10 and 20 and 30.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.750
So with that, I'd like you
to have a look at me, sort of

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.170
dealing with Indonesian in LingQ.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:10.489
We are, uh, I'm in Persian right now
doing this very interesting podcast in

00:03:10.489 --> 00:03:16.670
Persian, but I want to sort of experience
going to a language for the first time.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:20.989
So we're going to change the language
and we're going to go to something

00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:25.725
that I haven't done, practically,
haven't done anything on Indonesian.

00:03:25.725 --> 00:03:26.115
Okay.

00:03:26.115 --> 00:03:32.805
I got two words in Indonesian and I think
I spent some time just looking at it,

00:03:32.805 --> 00:03:35.205
just exploring it to see what's there.

00:03:35.805 --> 00:03:40.965
So in the library, I mean,
let's see my lessons.

00:03:41.505 --> 00:03:45.255
I guess I, I looked at the Mini Stories
and I looked at this other thing.

00:03:45.255 --> 00:03:49.215
I was curious, Buddha, but let's
go to the Mini Stories because

00:03:49.605 --> 00:03:51.435
starting a language from scratch.

00:03:52.155 --> 00:03:53.985
Uh, that's what I would do.

00:03:54.165 --> 00:04:00.585
So if we look at our statistics, 97%
of the words in here are new to me.

00:04:00.585 --> 00:04:03.675
The reason it's 97, because I
remember I did look at a lesson

00:04:04.245 --> 00:04:06.405
and looked up a few words.

00:04:07.425 --> 00:04:09.855
Uh, here is story one.

00:04:09.855 --> 00:04:13.515
So if we look up story one and I want
to point out something that I think is

00:04:13.515 --> 00:04:17.355
quite important, and which I mentioned
earlier is when you're starting in a

00:04:17.355 --> 00:04:19.305
language, you don't have to start at one.

00:04:19.305 --> 00:04:22.065
You don't have to start
necessarily at "how are you?"

00:04:22.455 --> 00:04:25.035
Uh, father, mother, sister, brother.

00:04:25.335 --> 00:04:30.075
Um, the present tense isn't
necessarily easier than the past tense.

00:04:30.145 --> 00:04:31.395
There is no real order.

00:04:31.965 --> 00:04:36.315
So I think we're conditioned to want
to go story one, story two, story

00:04:36.315 --> 00:04:38.625
three, but it doesn't really matter.

00:04:39.105 --> 00:04:45.675
Um, if I look at story one here, you
will see that I did spend some time,

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:49.260
I don't know when looking at
this, I looked at these words.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.270
I don't remember any of these words.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:58.760
I did this just as an experiment some time
ago, but what I'm going to do instead is

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:03.420
I'm going to go to the next lesson and
let's assume that we start on lesson two.

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:08.700
Some of the words that showed up in
lesson one will be here in lesson two.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:12.870
And so the whole thing about
starting from scratch is you just

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:14.700
want to discover the language.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:19.980
I could look up a grammar book
and get some hints as to how the

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:26.430
language works, uh, from, you know,
subject, uh, subject verb, noun or

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:27.760
whatever, but it doesn't matter.

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:28.680
We're just going to explore.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:32.970
So this first word ... means
following, next.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:33.540
Okay.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:40.745
... so the following is a story....

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:52.115
now this is a capital, so I'm assuming
it's a person's name ... and okay.

00:05:54.185 --> 00:05:54.565
... Okay.

00:05:54.565 --> 00:05:59.445
So you have to assume
that this is "winter".

00:06:02.635 --> 00:06:04.995
So I'm just going to
discover the language.

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:12.315
So the following is a story about
Puji and Liburan and her holiday...

00:06:12.315 --> 00:06:13.604
in her winter holidays.

00:06:13.635 --> 00:06:14.054
Okay.

00:06:14.265 --> 00:06:18.104
Now I have an advantage in doing
the Mini Stories in that I've done

00:06:18.104 --> 00:06:19.755
them in so many different languages.

00:06:20.314 --> 00:06:20.844
Okay.

00:06:21.184 --> 00:06:21.884
Puji...

00:06:26.244 --> 00:06:29.810
okay, she's obviously excited.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:43.890
... so this obviously is another way of
saying "winter" or "winter vacation"...

00:06:48.159 --> 00:07:05.705
so by looking these words up, I can
actually follow the meaning here.

00:07:06.245 --> 00:07:11.795
So what I would normally do, and I
should point out too, that in terms of

00:07:11.795 --> 00:07:18.185
dictionaries, I'm using Bob blah, I could
look up, you know, again, it depends

00:07:18.185 --> 00:07:20.495
what, what your native language is.

00:07:20.495 --> 00:07:24.375
You can choose from a variety of
addiction areas, some languages

00:07:24.375 --> 00:07:25.665
will have more than others.

00:07:26.175 --> 00:07:28.605
Uh, what does this say?

00:07:30.225 --> 00:07:34.945
So this looks to be a dictionary
entirely in Indonesian.

00:07:35.715 --> 00:07:37.575
May not be so meaningful for me.

00:07:37.605 --> 00:07:39.135
I don't think I'll use it again.

00:07:39.495 --> 00:07:41.085
Maybe at a later stage.

00:07:41.534 --> 00:07:42.765
So I close this.

00:07:43.455 --> 00:07:48.615
So I'm going to stay with, let's
see, what did Babla give me?

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:51.299
Yeah here.

00:07:51.359 --> 00:07:53.250
So this gives me more information.

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:56.789
I'm going to stay with
Babla and Google translate.

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:57.539
Okay.

00:07:57.599 --> 00:07:59.520
I can do one more sentence now.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:00.809
Of course, I can listen to this.

00:08:00.809 --> 00:08:05.729
In fact, what I would normally do,
I should point out is that I would

00:08:05.729 --> 00:08:07.469
go and do this in sentence mode.

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:17.479
That way I can look at
it sentence by sentence.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:20.370
Okay.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:25.125
So here I take this sentence and
I might spend some time there

00:08:25.155 --> 00:08:27.275
or I go to the next sentence.

00:08:27.965 --> 00:08:39.635
Uh, it says, uh, met my daily target.

00:08:39.965 --> 00:08:41.044
You can set your own target.

00:08:41.044 --> 00:08:52.585
I don't worry about those
things, but some people like it.

00:08:53.725 --> 00:08:54.265
Okay.

00:08:59.135 --> 00:09:17.670
So, I mean, I I'm going through these,
these words will all be status one and

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:19.500
I won't go through the whole thing.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:21.090
I mean, I can do the next sentence.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:21.630
Whoops.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:24.810
That wasn't what I wanted
to do, uh, wherever here.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:26.730
Okay.

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:32.960
So here again.

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:47.700
So I would normally, and I think
that's enough here of, of going

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:50.970
through the Mini Stories here.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:55.050
Uh, and, and let me explain a little
bit, uh, how I would use these Mini

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:59.070
Stories and how it eventually enables
me to break into the language.

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:00.620
All right.

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:06.530
So it gives you a bit of a sense that,
that I begin my contact with a new

00:10:06.530 --> 00:10:09.500
language by just discovering what's there.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.840
Uh, some languages have the
verb at the end, some languages

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:15.180
have the verb in the middle.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:20.340
And some books will tell you this, but
actually as you go through discovering

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:25.410
the language, translating each word,
if necessary, translating a phrase,

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:27.750
you start to discover the language.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:33.390
So if typically what I would do is I
would pick my way through one story.

00:10:34.185 --> 00:10:35.415
Then I would listen to it.

00:10:35.805 --> 00:10:39.944
Uh, I can listen to it and by the way,
I would do the story in sentence mode.

00:10:40.214 --> 00:10:42.944
Definitely it's much easier
to do in sentence mode.

00:10:43.305 --> 00:10:45.074
And then you can listen to each sentence.

00:10:45.525 --> 00:10:50.234
And after having worked your way
through and looked up words and what

00:10:50.234 --> 00:10:53.714
I didn't show in the demonstration
was using the flashcards.

00:10:53.984 --> 00:10:59.055
Typically I would, whenever I turn the
page, I would do some flash carding while

00:10:59.055 --> 00:11:02.114
those words are still fresh in my mind.

00:11:02.775 --> 00:11:06.555
And having done all of that, if
I then go away and listen to the

00:11:06.564 --> 00:11:08.745
story, I won't understand much.

00:11:08.834 --> 00:11:09.285
Okay.

00:11:09.824 --> 00:11:10.665
That's the reality.

00:11:10.964 --> 00:11:16.064
And so then I have to go back
and I read it again and pretty...

00:11:16.094 --> 00:11:20.265
and after reading it again and looking up
words again and going through flashcards

00:11:20.265 --> 00:11:25.574
again, uh, then I go and listen to
it and I still don't understand much.

00:11:26.745 --> 00:11:30.675
And, but then I will move
on to the next lesson.

00:11:31.035 --> 00:11:35.595
And I typically will go in sequence,
but you don't have to, uh, but it's

00:11:35.595 --> 00:11:38.295
better to go in sequence because
then you remember what you've done.

00:11:38.985 --> 00:11:42.545
Uh, so normally I would start with
lesson one and then lesson two

00:11:42.545 --> 00:11:44.025
and lesson three and so forth.

00:11:44.865 --> 00:11:49.095
And I'll understand 20% eventually,
you know, I'll start to understand

00:11:49.125 --> 00:11:55.185
10, 20, 30% I keep on doing this,
but I also, and in fact, it's been

00:11:55.185 --> 00:12:02.310
proven based on, you know, research
and cognitive processes in our brain,

00:12:02.819 --> 00:12:07.229
don't spend your whole time trying
to nail down lesson one as the

00:12:07.229 --> 00:12:09.449
efficiency declines very quickly.

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:13.229
Uh, rather you should move on
to the next lesson because even

00:12:13.319 --> 00:12:16.410
another lesson that you don't
understand is still a new lesson.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:18.240
The brain likes novelty.

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:21.689
So I typically will spend a fair
amount of time on lesson one,

00:12:21.959 --> 00:12:25.800
understand after a while, understand,
I understand more and more when reading

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:27.209
it with the help of a dictionary.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:30.390
Uh, listening without reading.

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:34.290
Uh, I understand very little, but
then I keep on moving forward.

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:37.380
Lesson two, lesson three,
lesson four, lesson five, then

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:38.819
I'll go back to lesson one.

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:44.790
And by the time you reach lesson 10,
lesson one, which you've now listened to

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:47.730
15 times, it's starting to make sense.

00:12:47.939 --> 00:12:52.050
And that's basically the process
combination of listening while

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:53.760
reading in sentence mode.

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:57.060
Uh, looking at words, looking
at words again and again.

00:12:57.435 --> 00:13:00.975
Uh, reviewing words and
flashcards after each page.

00:13:01.395 --> 00:13:04.785
And then of course, a lot of listening
away from it, not understanding an

00:13:04.785 --> 00:13:09.225
awful lot, but now you're more and
more curious, like, what is that

00:13:09.225 --> 00:13:11.145
meaning that I can't seem to remember?

00:13:11.445 --> 00:13:15.945
And you go by and all of a sudden,
as was the case for me with say

00:13:15.945 --> 00:13:19.635
Persian and Arabic, which have
a much more difficult script.

00:13:19.635 --> 00:13:24.135
I mean, Indonesian has the advantage
that it's written in the Latin alphabet.

00:13:24.495 --> 00:13:26.714
But I did this for Persian and Arabic.

00:13:27.105 --> 00:13:33.045
Now I will also get myself a book, Teach
Yourself, or, you know, Colloquial.

00:13:33.375 --> 00:13:37.005
Because it's, it's nice to cover
the same material in different ways.

00:13:37.125 --> 00:13:41.325
So I'll read through it and they'll
have some different monologues

00:13:41.325 --> 00:13:43.275
or dialogues per each lesson.

00:13:43.275 --> 00:13:46.905
And they'll explain some things about the
language, which might be useful to me.

00:13:46.905 --> 00:13:51.255
I won't fully be able to use those
explanations, but it all, it's all part

00:13:51.255 --> 00:13:54.705
of my gradually discovering the language.

00:13:55.185 --> 00:13:57.825
I should say that Indonesian
would be an attractive one to do.

00:13:57.825 --> 00:14:02.795
I'm not about to abandon Persian and
Arabic, but Indonesian is spoken by,

00:14:03.225 --> 00:14:06.975
I think if we add in Malaysia and
Indonesia, 200 million people.

00:14:07.305 --> 00:14:09.975
Uh, I think that are common
words with, uh, with...

00:14:10.485 --> 00:14:13.694
I'm not sure, uh, it's an
important part of the world.

00:14:13.694 --> 00:14:15.015
It's an important language.

00:14:15.525 --> 00:14:17.655
Uh, it's meant to be easy.

00:14:17.925 --> 00:14:22.095
Uh, I don't know if that's true
or not, but, uh, certainly the

00:14:22.095 --> 00:14:23.655
writing system is easy for us.

00:14:23.925 --> 00:14:25.845
Uh, there are no tones to deal with.

00:14:26.204 --> 00:14:33.800
So it's one that I may very well go and
learn later on, but I think it's, it's an

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:38.360
easy one to use as a demonstration of how
to start a language, uh, from scratch.

00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:41.990
And I can assure you, I use the
same process in much more difficult

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:44.180
languages like Persian and Arabic.

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:48.080
The important thing is not to get
worried about what you don't understand

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:52.640
trust the fact that the brain will
eventually start to form some patterns,

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:55.470
will eventually start to understand it.

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:59.700
It seems at first impossible, and
it seemed as, particularly for

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:05.040
me, uh, with the Persian, with the
Arabic script, you know, impossible.

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:07.230
Uh, but you just have to stay with it.

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:09.750
You just have to stay with
it and keep listening.

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:14.670
And I end up listening to these stories
30, 40, 50 times, but not all at once,

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:18.390
not listening to it over and over again,
trying to force myself to understand it.

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:23.115
I go through it and, and, and
continue, and then go back and and

00:15:23.115 --> 00:15:26.425
sometimes I'll listen to the, uh,
you know, there is a function at

00:15:26.425 --> 00:15:28.724
LingQ "listen to the course audio".

00:15:28.814 --> 00:15:30.625
So that'll be set up on my iPhone.

00:15:30.645 --> 00:15:31.574
I can listen to it.

00:15:31.755 --> 00:15:32.775
I can shuffle it.

00:15:33.165 --> 00:15:36.704
And slowly, slowly, slowly, all
of this starts to make sense.

00:15:36.974 --> 00:15:43.175
Once you have a base, as in say the Mini
Stories you can then branch off into

00:15:43.185 --> 00:15:47.444
more difficult, um, you know, authentic
content that you might run to import from

00:15:47.444 --> 00:15:49.515
somewhere, or you can find in our library.

00:15:50.084 --> 00:15:53.895
Uh, but I think the Mini Stories,
because there's so much repetition and,

00:15:53.895 --> 00:15:58.844
and remember, you don't need to ace the
most frequent 500 words, they're going

00:15:58.844 --> 00:16:01.275
to show up anyway, they will show up.

00:16:01.305 --> 00:16:02.055
Don't worry about it.

00:16:02.594 --> 00:16:06.435
Uh, you have to recognize that there
is going to be a long period where

00:16:06.435 --> 00:16:10.334
you have to acquire these, you know,
relatively less frequent words,

00:16:10.694 --> 00:16:14.295
uh, and which are very important
to the meaning of any context.

00:16:14.775 --> 00:16:17.775
Uh, however, the, the, the
Mini Stories is a place where

00:16:17.775 --> 00:16:19.245
I get a start in the language.

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:21.180
I get a sense of the language.

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:24.570
I get some basic vocabulary,
some basic phrasing.

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:30.210
Now I'm ready to start exploring
beyond that, but that's how the sort

00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:34.320
of listening and reading starting from
scratch when you know nothing about

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:36.180
the language, that's how I do it.

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:38.250
So I hope that was helpful.

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:41.910
And, uh, I, I'm going to offer
you a couple of videos that kind

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:45.390
of follow along in the same vein.

